& [JustificationIdentity] is the durable SJS evidence bridge
  + [justification_uuid] is 32c9df98-c226-4e59-8f35-f4f8356d777b
  + [claim] is the Cantor build is sufficiently defined to begin a deterministic first slice without further broad ideation
  + [confidence] is high for engine boundary, authority rules, query protocol shape, result proof, safe-Rust runtime, CLI ordering, and neural authority limits
  + [confidence] is moderate for parser coverage, production cryptography, persistence backend, routing corpus, model family, and adoption threshold

& [EvidenceSet] is support for the decision
  + [source_evidence] is the preserved request explicitly selects engine, neural fabric, CLI, parameterized subject and use-case search, semantic boundaries, descriptions, and SOP detail results
  + [fabric_evidence] is existing signed Cantor query, trust, execution, Rust, and neural-fabric profiles converge on one core protocol
  + [experiment_evidence] is the llama.cpp probe proves a native tool-call checkpoint but does not prove the Cantor engine
  + [governance_evidence] is Specification Explosion produced atomic requirements, inward-depth contracts, constraints, pruning, acceptance gates, and contiguous slices

& [DecisionSet] is the accepted architecture reasoning
  + [decision] is begin with deterministic semantic IR because truth-bearing identities, boundaries, and proof must be testable before learned routing
  + [decision] is compile admitted source into immutable package authority because live files and line numbers are unstable
  + [decision] is use an authoritative typed graph plus derived indexes because relation shape and lookup optimization have different trust roles
  + [decision] is expose a CLI first because it is the smallest provider-independent Codex-callable process boundary
  + [decision] is add MCP as a projection of the proven protocol rather than a second engine
  + [decision] is call the weighted or learned component a router because weights propose retrieval paths but do not contain or authorize SOP truth
  + [decision] is offer two future FPGA profiles: a stable bytecode interpreter for flexibility and a native synthesized circuit for measured stable hotspots
  + [decision] is use the Rust core as the semantic reference oracle and require normalized result and fault equivalence before hardware adoption

& [AlternativeDisposition] is the pruned option record
  + [rejected] is neural-network-first semantic storage because it makes exact meaning, signature authority, and proof difficult to recover
  + [rejected] is physical-database-first design because current query shapes and scale evidence are insufficient
  + [deferred] is production signing infrastructure until test-signing validates the package lifecycle
  + [deferred] is resident daemon until repeated CLI startup, reload, concurrency, or notification cost provides evidence
  + [outside] is model hidden-state modification because tool-call checkpoints already support the intended first seam
  + [deferred] is FPGA target selection and implementation until the software protocol, measured workload, device constraints, and hardware threat model exist

& [CounterargumentSet] is scrutiny retained
  + [counterargument] is the project may still be over-specified before the eight basic fixtures exist
  + [answer] is the canonical document authorizes only a small first slice and keeps mature layers dependency-gated
  + [counterargument] is deterministic graph retrieval may already be sufficient
  + [answer] is learned routing has no implementation authority unless benchmark evidence shows material benefit
  + [counterargument] is an SOP parser may force a language redesign
  + [answer] is slice_1 begins with a versioned minimal machine form and eight fixtures; broader grammar coverage remains local uncertainty

& [AcceptanceDecision] is accepted
  + [accepted_now] is slices 1 through 4 as dependency-gated implementation targets
  + [not_yet_accepted] is learned router implementation or adoption
  + [not_yet_accepted] is FPGA implementation or target adoption
  + [first_action] is implement the Cargo workspace, semantic IR, and eight CoreAcceptance fixture proofs
  + [invalidation_condition] is source change, failed fixture proof, unresolvable authority model, protocol contradiction, or evidence that the chosen dependency order increases risk
